If you want to use `addEventListener` instead of inline event handlers (`onclick` or `onchange`), here’s how you can modify the previous examples:

---

### **1. Change Selected Option Using `addEventListener`**
```html
<select id="cars">
    <option value="bmw">BMW</option>
    <option value="audi">Audi</option>
    <option value="tesla">Tesla</option>
</select>

<button id="selectAudi">Select Audi</button>

<script>
    document.getElementById("selectAudi").addEventListener("click", function () {
        document.getElementById("cars").value = "audi";
    });
</script>
```
**👉 What it does:** Clicking the button selects "Audi" in the dropdown.

---

### **2. Add a New Option Using `addEventListener`**
```html
<select id="fruits">
    <option value="apple">Apple</option>
    <option value="banana">Banana</option>
</select>

<button id="addFruit">Add Orange</button>

<script>
    document.getElementById("addFruit").addEventListener("click", function () {
        let select = document.getElementById("fruits");
        let newOption = document.createElement("option");
        newOption.value = "orange";
        newOption.textContent = "Orange";
        select.appendChild(newOption);
    });
</script>
```
**👉 What it does:** Clicking the button adds "Orange" to the dropdown.

---

### **3. Remove an Option Using `addEventListener`**
```html
<select id="subjects">
    <option value="math">Math</option>
    <option value="science">Science</option>
    <option value="history">History</option>
</select>

<button id="removeSubject">Remove Last Option</button>

<script>
    document.getElementById("removeSubject").addEventListener("click", function () {
        let select = document.getElementById("subjects");
        if (select.options.length > 0) {
            select.remove(select.options.length - 1);
        }
    });
</script>
```
**👉 What it does:** Clicking the button removes the last option in the dropdown.

---

### **4. Detect When User Changes Selection Using `addEventListener`**
```html
<select id="animals">
    <option value="dog">Dog</option>
    <option value="cat">Cat</option>
    <option value="lion">Lion</option>
</select>

<p id="output"></p>

<script>
    document.getElementById("animals").addEventListener("change", function () {
        let select = document.getElementById("animals");
        document.getElementById("output").textContent = "You selected: " + select.value;
    });
</script>
```
**👉 What it does:** When the user selects a new animal, it displays the selected option.

---

### **Why Use `addEventListener` Instead of Inline Events?**
✅ It **separates JavaScript from HTML**, making code cleaner.  
✅ It **allows multiple event listeners** on the same element.  
✅ It **follows best practices** for modern JavaScript.  

