If you want to use `change` instead of `click`, it means the event will trigger **when the user selects a new option in the dropdown** instead of clicking a button. Here's how you can modify the previous examples:  

---

### **1. Change Selected Option and Detect the Change**  
```html
<select id="cars">
    <option value="bmw">BMW</option>
    <option value="audi">Audi</option>
    <option value="tesla">Tesla</option>
</select>

<p id="output"></p>

<script>
    document.getElementById("cars").addEventListener("change", function () {
        document.getElementById("output").textContent = "You selected: " + this.value;
    });
</script>
```
**👉 What it does:** Whenever the user selects a different car, it updates the paragraph with the selected option.

---

### **2. Add a New Option When Selection Changes**  
```html
<select id="fruits">
    <option value="apple">Apple</option>
    <option value="banana">Banana</option>
</select>

<script>
    document.getElementById("fruits").addEventListener("change", function () {
        let select = document.getElementById("fruits");
        if (select.value === "banana") {
            let newOption = document.createElement("option");
            newOption.value = "orange";
            newOption.textContent = "Orange";
            select.appendChild(newOption);
        }
    });
</script>
```
**👉 What it does:** If the user selects "Banana", it adds "Orange" to the dropdown.

---

### **3. Remove an Option When Selection Changes**  
```html
<select id="subjects">
    <option value="math">Math</option>
    <option value="science">Science</option>
    <option value="history">History</option>
</select>

<script>
    document.getElementById("subjects").addEventListener("change", function () {
        let select = document.getElementById("subjects");
        if (select.value === "history") {
            select.remove(select.selectedIndex);
        }
    });
</script>
```
**👉 What it does:** If the user selects "History", it removes it from the dropdown.

---

### **4. Enable/Disable a Button Based on Selection**
```html
<select id="payment">
    <option value="">Select Payment Method</option>
    <option value="credit">Credit Card</option>
    <option value="paypal">PayPal</option>
</select>

<button id="payButton" disabled>Proceed to Pay</button>

<script>
    document.getElementById("payment").addEventListener("change", function () {
        let button = document.getElementById("payButton");
        button.disabled = this.value === "";
    });
</script>
```
**👉 What it does:** The "Proceed to Pay" button is disabled until the user selects a payment method.

---

### **Why Use `change` Instead of `click`?**
✅ Triggers **only when the user selects a different option** (not when clicking a button).  
✅ More **efficient for dropdown menus** where selection matters.  
✅ Helps in **form validation** by checking user input dynamically.  

